<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaswant Singh of Marwar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1626">1626</a>–<a href="/years/1678">1678</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jaswant Singh I of Jodhpur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaswant Singh was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> ruler of Marwar, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, from 1638 to 1678 and a senior commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, campaigning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and on the north-west frontier, where he died. When Aurangzeb tried to take over Marwar after his death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durgadas-rathore">Durgadas Rathore</a> led a long resistance on behalf of his posthumous son Ajit Singh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaswant-singh-of-marwar.jpg" alt="Jaswant Singh of Marwar" width="220" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaswant_Singh_of_Marwar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jaswant Singh was born on 26 December 1626 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>, the youngest son of Maharaja Gaj Singh of Marwar. He succeeded on 6 May 1638 by special decree of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> and was installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> on 25 May 1638. His father's favourite concubine, Anara Begum, who regarded him as her own son and was at odds with the heir apparent Amar Singh Rathore, had a part in his accession; Amar Singh was later given Nagore in compensation. He held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> the parganas of Jodhpur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siwana">Siwana</a>, Merta, Sojat, Phalodi and Pokharan, and Shah Jahan granted him the personal title of Maharaja on 6 January 1654.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> sent him to stop the advance of the rebel princes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and Murad. The armies met at Dharmatpur, fifteen miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, on 15 April 1658. His advisers proposed a night raid on Aurangzeb's artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> army was almost wholly light cavalry; he is quoted as answering that it was inconsistent with the manliness of the Rajputs to employ stratagems or make a night attack. He was defeated and lost 6,000 men, among them Ratan Singh Rathore of Ratlam and Mokand Das Hada of Kota.</p><p>Posted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> his headquarters. In 1664 he besieged Kondana without success, and he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> after Jai Singh was sent to the Deccan. He returned in 1667 with Prince Muazzam, the new viceroy, and acted as an intermediary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. In 1670 he was posted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son Prithviraj Singh died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 8 May 1667; the khyats, the chronicles of Marwar, say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> had given him a poisoned robe. Jaswant Singh died at Jamrud, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, on a date usually converted as 28 December 1678, though another scholar makes it 28 November 1678. Two of his wives were pregnant at his death and both bore sons, which led to a war over the installation of Ajit Singh Rathore as ruler of Marwar. He was also a man of letters, the author of the Siddhant-Bodh, the Anand Vilas and the Bhasha-Bhushan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaswant Singh of Marwar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Rathore princes became Mughal commanders, but when Aurangzeb tried to take Marwar after <b>Jaswant Singh</b>'s death in 1678, Durgadas Rathore led a long resistance.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Jamrud, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.29,73.03&amp;year=1659" aria-label="Jodhpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6478 1198 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jaswant Singh of Marwar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6928 1468h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6928 1468h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.29,73.03&amp;year=1659">What was here in 1659? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
